### Access the JupyterLab application

`jd open` launches the local client proxy and opens your browser to the loopback address the proxy binds
(e.g. `http://127.0.0.1:PORT/lab`). The browser talks plain HTTP to the loopback proxy, which forwards to
the instance over pinned self-signed TLS and injects a short-lived AWS-identity token on every request —
there is **no browser sign-in**. `jd open` runs in the foreground by default (Ctrl-C stops it); pass
-d/--detached to run it in the background.

{{ single-server-open-instructions }}

The `jd proxy` command group drives the proxy directly:

```bash
# start the proxy in the background, then open a tab against it
jd proxy start
jd proxy open

# inspect it
jd proxy status
jd proxy show --json

# stop it
jd proxy stop
```

The client proxy ships as an optional extra — install it with `pip install "jupyter-deploy[proxy]"`.

